[["Corsica","3779","mrgid","",""],["France","17","mrgid","",""],["Italy","2133","mrgid","",""],["Spain","2185","mrgid","",""]]},"GVC9":{"d":[["Sumatra","","text","",""]]},"K84B":{"d":[["United States Exclusive Economic Zone (Puerto Rico)","33179","mrgid","native",""],["United States Exclusive Economic Zone (Puerto Rico)","33179","mrgid","native",""]]},"KFVR":{"d":[["Eastern Atlantic Ocean","","text","",""]]},"KG5R":{"d":[["Venezuelan part of the Caribbean Sea","25306","mrgid","",""]]},"KJNV":{"d":[["","CBD","tdwg","",""],["","VIE","tdwg","",""]]},"KQT63":{"d":[["Distribution. Known from New Caledonia, South Fiji Ridge and now off northern New Zealand (Fig. 8). Bathymetric range 282 – 1089 m (Ho & Shao, 2010); specimens from the present study area taken at 395 – 920 m. Remarks. Five specimens collected from northern New Zealand represent the first record for the area; one of these (NMNZ P. 005525) was originally reported by Stephenson (1971) as C. pictus. It is notable that no specimens exceeding 100 mm SL were found in New Zealand waters, whereas adults attain 271 mm SL elsewhere (Ho & Shao, 2010).","","text","",""]]},"KZDYW":{"d":[["Florida","48141","mrgid","",""]]},"L23QW":{"d":[["Peru","48398","mrgid","",""]]},"L2QTV":{"d":[["Taiwan","","text","native",""]]},"L8W7G":{"d":[["North Carolina","8929","mrgid","native",""],["United States","2204","mrgid","native",""]]},"LGR4":{"d":[["Europe & Northern Asia (excluding China)","","text","",""]]},"LHYBJ":{"d":[["Southern Asia","","text","",""]]},"LP6Q6":{"d":[["Distribution in Iran. Golestan (Heiss 2004), Guilan (Heiss 2004), Mazandaran (Hoberlandt 1974 (as crenatus); Kerzhner & Rieger 1985; Heiss 2004). General distribution. Widely distributed in Armenia, Azerbaijan, Bielorussia, Georgia, Southern Russia, Turkmenistan, Ukraine.","","text","",""]]},"LPXL9":{"d":[["Distribution (Fig. 186) and biology. Aulacophora relicta is a widespread and common species of northern Australia and eastern New South Wales with old records for South Australia, Victoria and Tasmania. The latter may represent occasional incursions from the north which do not form sustainable populations, or may simply reflect lack of recent collecting in these areas. The distribution of A. relicta in northern Australia encloses both A. barrogae and A. mbabaram and overlaps slightly with A. abdominalis in the Torres Straits, occurring as far northeast as Darnley Island, 200 km northwest of mainland Australia. Given that Biosecurity measures have not restricted movement of cucurbit plants north from the mainland, it is quite possible that some Torres Straits specimens found are due to recent manmade movements, with long term establishment unknown. Aulacophora relicta has been collected in every month of the year. Aulacophora relicta has been recorded damaging pumpkins (Cucurbita maxima) and “ all commercial cucurbit crops ” in Australia, under the name A. abdominalis (Waterhouse & Norris 1987; Napier 2009; Brown 2015). Few of the specimens we examined had any host data, with only two records for Citrullus sativa, one for C. lanatus, three for Cucumis melo, and two for Cucurbita pepo, all of which are exotic. One adult is labelled as feeding on the seed of an exotic weed in the Asteraceae (Xanthium species). However native Cucurbitaceae species occur throughout the range of A. relicta and these are probably the native hosts.","","text","",""]]},"LQ435":{"d":[["Distribution. South-eastern Turkey (province of Hakkâri).","","text","",""]]},"M5Z6G":{"d":[["Norway","NO","iso","",""],["Norway","NO","iso","",""]]},"M6ZRJ":{"d":[["Distribution. Ecuador and Peru (Fig. 16).","","text","",""]]},"M9KH":{"d":[["Mexico (Chiapas, Colima, Guerrero, Hidalgo, Jalisco, México State, Michoacán, Oaxaca, Puebla, Querétaro, San Luis Potosí, Tabasco, Tamaulipas, Veracruz); Guatemala; Belize; El Salvador; Honduras; Nicaragua; Costa Rica; W-Panama; Colombia (Antioquia, Cundinamarca, Meta, Nariño); Venezuela (Merida); Ecuador; Peru; Bolivia (Chuquisaca, La Paz, Santa Cruz); SE-Brazil (Minas Gerais, São Paulo)","","text","",""]]},"MDTT":{"d":[["","COS-OO","tdwg","native",""],["","PAN-OO","tdwg","native",""],["Costa Rica, Panama","","text","native",""]]},"MHM7":{"d":[["European waters (ERMS scope)","7130","mrgid","",""]]},"MK7YQ":{"d":[["Distribution. this species was described and hitherto known only from Greece.","","text","",""]]},"MT5C":{"d":[["Canada","","text","native",""],["USA","","text","native",""]]},"N6NG":{"d":[["European waters (ERMS scope)","7130","mrgid","",""]]},"N95PM":{"d":[["Type locality. “ Region of Mt Pratt, in the north-eastern area of the Delta Division of Papua ” (Troughton & Le Souef, 1936 a: 195). Locality data considered erroneous by Kirsch & Calaby (1977). Groves (1982) also rejected Mt Pratt as the origin of the type material, which he believed came from the Huon Peninsula. Helgen et al. (2011: 250) discussed this entity at length and concluded that Mt Pratt could be an eastern outlier of Mt Bosavi, Southern Highlands Province, PNG.","","text","",""]]},"N9T2L":{"d":[["New Caledonia (NC-New Caledonia)","","text","",""]]},"NB6S":{"d":[["Pakistan, India, Sri Lanka, China (Yongxing Is. = Woody Is.), Philippines, Indonesia (Sulawesi, Banda Is.). Introduced to North, Central, South America, Gambia, Sierra Leone, Seychelles, Yemen, Australia (Queensland), Pacific Is.","","text","",""]]},"NB72":{"d":[["Sri Lanka","","text","",""]]},"NSKTY":{"d":[["Distribution (Fig. 241). Costa Rica, Panama, Venezuela and Ecuador.","","text","",""]]},"P7R4L":{"d":[["DISTRIBUTION. Bangladesh. Map 2.","","text","",""]]},"PCN8":{"d":[["","ECU","tdwg","",""]]},"PHY2V":{"d":[["","TW","iso","native",""]]},"PZ3HT":{"d":[["CD, Orientale","","text","native",""]]},"QQH5Z":{"d":[["MRQ","MRQ","tdwg","",""]]},"QQH69":{"d":[["PF","PF","iso","",""]]},"RFKLJ":{"d":[["Southern Asia","","text","",""]]},"SBQHK":{"d":[["Southern Asia","","text","",""]]},"SKN49":{"d":[["Sweden","SE","iso","native",""]]},"SNWWJ":{"d":[["Distribut